EXT. TIDE POOLS - DAWN

A rocky shelf slick with kelp, exposed at the edge of a retreating sea. Mist clings to the water. Gulls wheel overhead. Pools lie scattered like dropped mirrors, each holding a patch of pink sky.

NOOR HADDAD-PETERS, sixteen, kneels at the lip of the nearest pool in a yellow raincoat too big for her. She is small, serious, hair braided tightly against the wind. A notebook is wrapped in a freezer bag at her knee. She watches the water as if it might speak.

FIONA ESCOBAR-LINDQVIST, sixteen, stands a few feet back, arms folded. She wears a grey hoodie and borrowed rubber boots. Her eyes keep drifting from the pool to the horizon and back to Noor.

She shifts her weight. A pebble clicks under her heel. Noor does not look up.

She is the one who brought Fiona here. She is the one who won't say why.

FIONA
You said there was something to see.

NOOR
Wait.

FIONA
It's freezing.

NOOR
She said wait.

FIONA
Who's "she"?

Noor does not answer. She is still looking at the water. A ripple moves under the surface, slow, purposeful. She holds her breath. Then, quite deliberately, she reaches out and slides a finger into the pool.

An anemone opens around it, soft pink petals curling, and closes again, gentle as a handshake.

NOOR (CONT'D)
There.

Fiona edges closer. She crouches. She stares, unblinking, at the little flower of tentacles.

FIONA
It's alive.

NOOR
It's alive.

FIONA
It's... holding on.

NOOR
She does that when she's afraid.

FIONA
Or when she's happy.

NOOR
Maybe both.

She looks up at last. The two girls stare at each other across the water. The mist breaks. A shaft of sunlight lances across the pool and turns it gold.

FIONA
You brought me here to show me this.

NOOR
Yes.

FIONA
Why?

NOOR
Because you said nothing ever stays.

Fiona's mouth tightens. She wipes her eyes with the back of her sleeve, angry at herself.

FIONA
That's not fair.

NOOR
It isn't.

FIONA
You used a sea creature to make a point.

NOOR
It worked.

Fiona laughs. A wet, startled sound. She sits back on the rock, boots splashing, and the anemone opens again in the widening ripples.

She wipes her nose and does not look at Noor. She does not need to.

The tide begins to turn. Water laps at the shelf's far edge, patient and inevitable. The two of them sit on, sharing the cold, and the light climbs.
